\section{Introduction}
Foodo (Guang; ISO 639-3 \texttt{fod}) is a Guang language spoken mainly around S\`em\`er\`e in northern Benin, a ``linguistic island''. It is written in the Latin alphabet, though few speakers are literate in it. Many Foodo speak Tem as a trade language. Published research is limited.

\section{Primary Educational Resources}
No primers or textbooks were found in the search results.

\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Plunkett, ``An overview of Foodo, a linguistic island in Benin'', \emph{Journal of West African Languages} 36(1--2): \url{https://journalofwestafricanlanguages.org/downloads/send/105-volume-36-number-1-2/540-an-overview-of-foodo-a-linguistic-island-in-benin}
\item Glottolog record for Plunkett (2009): \url{https://glottolog.org/resource/reference/id/551530}
\item Wikipedia: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foodo_language}
\end{itemize}

\section{Religious and Community Resources}
Omniglot notes that parts of the Bible have been translated into Foodo (not independently verified): \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/foodo.htm}. Joshua Project people profile: \url{https://www.joshuaproject.net/index.php/people_groups/11223/BN}

\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
Omniglot reports use of Foodo on the radio; no archive or recordings were found. The Omniglot page also hosts an alphabet chart.

\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Foodo has national-language status in Benin and a Latin-script writing system, but little literacy and no school materials were found. A curriculum would need to begin with orthography consolidation and primer development, using the Plunkett grammar sketch as a linguistic base.
\end{document}
